{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-88656b1fbd3e2732", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tA Dual Evaluation for\n2\tMusic Transcription\n3\tRESEARCH ARTICLE\n4\tPING WANG\n5\t\n6\tarXiv:2608.04511v1 [cs.SD] 5 Aug 2026\n7\t\n8\tGUANG YANG\n9\tNAZIF CAN TAMER\n10\tVICTORIA EBERT\n11\tNOAH A. SMITH\n12\t*Author affiliations can be found in the back matter of this article\n13\t\n14\tABSTRACT\n15\tAutomatic music transcription systems produce sheet music that can be read and\n16\tplayed back. We argue that these two targets call for complementary evaluations of\n17\tnotation similarity to a reference score and playback similarity to the original performance, respectively. Our study considers notation similarity metrics from the optical music recognition literature and a wide range of playback-similarity methods validated through a listening study across over 100 participants and 230 piano recordings covering 23 works, 30 performers, and six composers. We find, fortuitously, that\n18\tthe playback similarity metric that correlates best with human judgments, CLEWS, is\n19\talso the cheapest to run. We also find that the two evaluation dimensions favor different systems among a collection of 24 pipelines formed by pairing eight audio-toMIDI models with three MIDI-to-score converters, with the latter component systematically determining the favored objective. The complementarity between metrics also\n20\tholds when adding to the pool Rubato, a new end-to-end system that offers substantially improved notation similarity while remaining competitive, though not the best,\n21\ton playback similarity.\n22\t\n23\tKEYWORDS:\n24\tautomatic music transcription,\n25\tdual evaluation, evaluation\n26\tmethodology, human\n27\tlistening study, notation\n28\tsimilarity, playback similarity\n29\t\n30\t\f2\n31\t\n32\t1 INTRODUCTION\n33\tMusic is primarily experienced through two principal\n34\tmodalities: sight and sound. For many musical genres, engraved musical notation (sheet music) communicates performance instructions through spatial layout and notation structure. When performers play back\n35\tthe sheet music, they render the symbolic notation into\n36\tsound experienced by the listener. Importantly, no two\n37\tmusicians will play the same sheet music the same way.\n38\tDifferences in tempo, dynamics, and timing will be audible but not visible in conventional visual notation. If a\n39\tpiece of sheet music were always played identically by\n40\tevery performer, there would be no need to ever record\n41\tversions other than the original. If sheet music encoded\n42\tevery difference between performances, it would likely be\n43\tunreadable by musicians.\n44\tIn this work, we evaluate the tradeoff in notational and\n45\tplayback fidelity of 25 complete automatic music transcription (AMT) systems — 24 modular pipelines and one\n46\tend-to-end model — and highlight the importance of a\n47\tmulti-view evaluation of AMT.\n48\tAMT has been decomposed into subtasks, including\n49\tbeat tracking (Foscarin et al., 2024), audio-to-MIDI note\n50\tdetection (Bittner et al., 2022; Bradshaw et al., 2024;\n51\tGardner et al., 2022; Hawthorne et al., 2021; Kong et al.,\n52\t2021; Simon et al., 2022; Yan et al., 2021; Yan and Duan,\n53\t2024), and MIDI-to-score conversion (Beyer and Dai,\n54\t2024; Cuthbert and Ariza, 2010; MuseScore, 2021). These\n55\tsubtasks emphasize different parts of the audio-to-score\n56\tpipeline: audio-to-MIDI systems focus on recovering performed musical events from sound, while MIDI-to-score\n57\tsystems focus on converting symbolic events into readable notation. However, evaluating a complete audio-toscore system, as opposed to a component, requires considering both how the output appears as a written musical document and how it sounds when rendered back to\n58\taudio.\n59\tWe therefore formulate audio-to-score transcription\n60\tevaluation as a two-dimensional problem. The first\n61\tdimension seeks notation similarity: the generated sheet\n62\tmusic should closely match the reference sheet music\n63\tas a written musical document. The second dimension\n64\tseeks playback similarity: audio rendered from the generated sheet music should faithfully reproduce the music\n65\tin the original performance recording. These dimensions\n66\tare complementary: transcribed sheet music with high\n67\tnotation similarity may still have low playback similarity when rendered (e.g., because of an expressive performance), while a transcription with high playback similarity may offer a symbolic representation with low notation\n68\tsimilarity or poor readability. The examples in Figure 1\n69\tillustrate this tension.\n70\tTo study this tradeoff between notational similarity and playback similarity, we evaluate 24 complete audio-to-score transcription pipelines formed by\n71\t\n72\tcombining eight competitive and widely used audioto-MIDI models with three MIDI-to-score converters.\n73\tThese components include recent transformer-based\n74\tsystems, piano-specific transcription models, lightweight\n75\ttranscription baselines, and commonly used notationconversion tools, giving broad coverage of strongest publicly available audio-to-score pipelines. For notation similarity, we compare generated MusicXML scores against\n76\treference scores using OMR Normalized Edit Distance\n77\t(OMR-NED; Martinez-Sevilla et al., 2025), a normalized\n78\tedit-distance metric designed for symbolic music notation comparison. For playback similarity, we render each\n79\tgenerated score back to audio and compare it with the\n80\toriginal recording. This playback-side evaluation includes\n81\tdigital signal processing (DSP)-based feature-alignment\n82\tmetrics, dynamic time warping (DTW) (Sakoe and Chiba,\n83\t1978) and time warp edit distance (TWED) (Marteau,\n84\t2009), applied to multiple frame-level audio representations spanning pitch-class and harmonic, spectral, and\n85\trhythm and onset information, thereby capturing complementary aspects of pitch, harmony, timbre, onset\n86\tactivity, tempo, and temporal structure; human ABX\n87\tlistening judgments; pairwise AudioLM judges; and\n88\tlearned embedding-similarity evaluators.\n89\tBy considering notational fidelity as a separate dimension from playback fidelity, we cast light on the practical tradeoff between them: different transcription systems, and the developers who build them, may reasonably choose to optimize for different uses, from producing clean, musician-readable scores to capturing the\n90\tdetails needed for faithful audio resynthesis or performance analysis.\n91\tOur contributions are:\n92\t• We formulate audio-to-score transcription evaluation\n93\tas a two-dimensional problem seeking both notation\n94\tand playback similarity.\n95\t• We evaluate 24 complete audio-to-score pipelines\n96\tthat produce MusicXML scores from audio recordings,\n97\tseparating the effects of eight audio-to-MIDI models\n98\tand three MIDI-to-score converters.\n99\t• We collect a human ABX listening reference with 106\n100\tparticipants and 3,180 pairwise judgments, and use it\n101\tto validate DTW, TWED, and AudioLM judges, and\n102\tlearned embedding evaluators, including CLaMP 3\n103\tand CLEWS.\n104\t• We show that notation-side and playback-side\n105\tevaluation can prefer different converter families,\n106\tmotivating multi-view evaluation and tradeoffs in\n107\tsystem design.\n108\t• We evaluate a contemporaneously developed\n109\tend-to-end transcription system, Rubato (Tamer\n110\tet al., 2026) in the same framework.\n111\t\n112\t\f3\n113\t\n114\tFigure 1 Tradeoff between notation and playback similarity in audio-to-score transcription. The top row is a four-measure reference\n115\texcerpt from Beethoven, Piano Sonata No. 6, third movement, Op. 10 No. 2. Candidate A is produced by Aria-AMT (Bradshaw et al.,\n116\t2024) + MIDI2ScoreTransformer (Beyer and Dai, 2024) and Candidate B by ismir2021 (Hawthorne et al., 2021) +\n117\tMuseScore (MuseScore, 2021). Candidate A is more similar to the reference as written notation (OMR-NED = 49.3) but less similar in\n118\trendered playback (TWED cost = 1.260); Candidate B is the opposite (OMR-NED = 98.1, TWED cost = 0.322). Lower OMR-NED and\n119\tlower TWED are better. Notation-side and playback-side evaluation favor different outputs.\n120\t\n121\t2 METHODOLOGY\n122\tOur proposed evaluation setup distinguishes two primary\n123\tdimensions: notation similarity (§2.1) and playback similarity (§2.2).\n124\t\n125\tLet Ŷ denote a transcription and Y the reference sheet\n126\t̂ Y) and D(Y,\n127\t̂ Y) be the numbers of symmusic, and let I(Y,\n128\tbol insertions and deletions in the cheapest transformation of Ŷ into Y. The per-score OMR-NED is\n129\t̂ Y) =\n130\tOMR-NED(Y,\n131\t\n132\t2.1 NOTATION SIMILARITY\n133\tOur notation-side evaluation treats the generated sheet\n134\tmusic as a visual, written musical document. We use\n135\tOMR Normalized Edit Distance (OMR-NED) (MartinezSevilla et al., 2025), a metric developed for optical\n136\tmusic recognition (OMR) evaluation. OMR-NED compares\n137\texplicit notation objects in the predicted and reference\n138\tsheet music. Although its input here is a symbolic score\n139\trather than a page image, its target is the visual notation\n140\tdomain: it asks whether the symbols that would appear\n141\ton the page match the reference notation. OMR-NED is\n142\thence the best available operationalization of the kind of\n143\tnotation similarity sought by AMT; see §2.1.1 for a discussion of alternatives.\n144\tOMR-NED defines edit operations over music-notation\n145\tsymbols: note and rest properties such as pitch, accidentals, note heads, beams and ties, and non-note objects\n146\tsuch as clefs, key and time signatures, dynamics, slurs\n147\tand lyrics. It therefore measures differences in the sheet\n148\tmusic that would require manual correction by a user of\n149\tan OMR (or AMT) system.\n150\t\n151\t̂ Y) + D(Y,\n152\t̂ Y)\n153\tI(Y,\n154\t,\n155\t̂ + |Y|\n156\t|Y|\n157\t\n158\t1\n159\t\n160\twhere | ⋅ | counts notation symbols, so the metric is\n161\tlength-aware. Lower is better and zero is an exact match.\n162\tFollowing Martinez-Sevilla et al. (2025), we report the\n163\tmicro-average over evaluated files,\n164\t∑\n165\tOverall OMR-NED =\n166\t\n167\tk\n168\t\n169\t∑\n170\tI(Ŷ k , Yk ) + k D(Ŷ k , Yk )\n171\t,\n172\t∑ ̂\n173\t∑\n174\t|Yk | + k |Yk |\n175\tk\n176\t\n177\t2\n178\t\n179\twhere k indexes evaluated recording and sheet-music\n180\tpairs. For readability, all reported OMR-NED values, in both\n181\ttables and figures, are scaled by 100. The full symbol\n182\tinventory and the treatment of substitutions are given in\n183\tSupplementary File, Appendix A.1.\n184\t\n185\t2.1.1 Related work: AMT evaluation\n186\tMost automatic music transcription evaluation is\n187\treference-based: a system output is compared with a\n188\tground-truth representation, and the metric defines\n189\twhich differences matter. For audio-to-MIDI transcription, this comparison is usually made over frame-level\n190\tpitch activations or note events, using precision, recall,\n191\tand F-measure over estimated pitches, onsets, and\n192\toffsets (Raffel et al., 2014). These metrics are appropriate when the target output is MIDI-like note events.\n193\tHowever, for transcription that produces sheet music as\n194\tits final output, evaluation must consider whether the\n195\t\n196\t\f4\n197\t\n198\tresulting notation is musically interpretable and visually\n199\tusable.\n200\tThree notation-oriented metrics are the closest alternatives to OMR-NED. Cogliati and Duan (2017) count\n201\tnotation errors across dimensions such as notes, durations, rests, barlines and staff assignment, and map them\n202\tto human ratings with a regression fitted on a small rated\n203\tset; the counts are not disjoint, so one structural mistake\n204\tcan be penalised several times. MV2H (McLeod, 2019;\n205\tMcLeod and Steedman, 2018) enforces disjoint penalties over five dimensions, but targets musical structure\n206\trather than engraving, and its MusicXML workflow compares MIDI-derived events rather than notation objects.\n207\tMUSTER (Hiramatsu et al., 2021; Nakamura et al., 2018)\n208\treports separate error rates, of which only five noteoriented ones enter its MeanER.\n209\tWe applied MUSTER to the same sheet music. Its subscores do not support a consistent interpretation (Supplementary File, Table A.1): the structure-oriented VoiceER,\n210\tHandER and ScaleErr track OMR-NED (𝜌 = 0.94, 0.81,\n211\t0.81), whereas MeanER and OnsetER track human playback preference far more closely (𝜌 = 0.79 and 0.84) than\n212\tOMR-NED (𝜌 = 0.38 and 0.50). Choosing a different subscore therefore changes the conclusion, while MeanER\n213\tomits score-organization properties central to the visual\n214\ttask. We use OMR-NED for the main notation comparison;\n215\tfull MUSTER scores and ranks are in Supplementary File,\n216\tTables A.4 and A.5, and the three metrics are discussed\n217\tat length in Supplementary File, Appendix A.3.\n218\t\n219\tlearned bidirectional model; we therefore do not expect\n220\tthat using MuseScore’s rendering abilities in this way\n221\tgives any advantage to evaluated pipelines that use MuseScore for MIDI-to-score conversion. The rendered audio\n222\tis produced at 44.1 kHz and peak-normalized.\n223\t\n224\t2.2.2 Audio feature representations\n225\tPlayback similarity is computed over frame-level\n226\taudio features. Because transcription quality is\n227\tmultidimensional—pitch, timing, rhythm, duration, and\n228\tharmony—and no single feature captures all of it, we\n229\tevaluate fourteen features (Bogdanov et al., 2013; McFee\n230\tet al., 2015) in three groups: pitch-class and harmonic\n231\t(Chroma, HPCP, Chroma CENS, Tonnetz, CQT, STFT semitone, pitch salience), capturing note content, harmonic\n232\tstructure and tonal motion; spectral (Mel spectrogram,\n233\tMFCC, PCEN-Mel, Log-STFT, spectral flux), which can be\n234\tsensitive to rendering artifacts; and rhythm/onset (onset\n235\tstrength, tempogram). Each feature is listed with its\n236\tcitation and musical property in Supplementary File,\n237\tTable A.7.\n238\tAll reference and rendered audio is processed at\n239\t44.1 kHz. Frame-based features use a 10 ms hop with\n240\tframe-wise normalization before distances are computed, and tempogram is derived from onset strength.\n241\tEach representation is scored independently: per feature\n242\twe compute one reference–candidate DTW or TWED distance per recording and average over the evaluation set\n243\tto obtain one system-level score. The per-feature scores\n244\tare compared in §4.4.\n245\t\n246\t2.2 PLAYBACK SIMILARITY\n247\tWe treat playback similarity as the second evaluation\n248\tdimension measured through multiple evaluator families. We first render each transcribed score to audio\n249\t(§2.2.1) and extract frame-level audio representations\n250\tfrom the original and rendered recordings (§2.2.2).\n251\tWe then compare the recordings using deterministic\n252\tDSP-based feature-alignment metrics, DTW and TWED\n253\t(§2.2.3), and learned or model-based evaluators, including AudioLM judges, CLaMP 3, and CLEWS (§2.2.4). We\n254\tvalidate these automatic evaluators against human pairwise listening judgments in §4.4 and compare their practical costs in §4.5.\n255\t\n256\t2.2.1 Audio rendering\n257\tAudio-based evaluation requires converting symbolic\n258\trepresentation into audio. Therefore, for each system, we\n259\tconvert the predicted MusicXML score to MIDI and then\n260\tto waveform audio. We use the same rendering setup\n261\tacross systems.\n262\tThe rendering pipeline uses MuseScore to export\n263\tMusicXML files to MIDI, followed by FluidSynth (FluidSynth\n264\tContributors, 2022) to synthesize WAV audio using the\n265\tSGM soundfont (Shan, 2024). MuseScore is used here only\n266\tas a fixed downstream renderer, and its relevant import/export routines are rule-based rather than a shared\n267\t\n268\t2.2.3 Feature-alignment playback metrics\n269\tAfter extracting frame-level audio features, we compare each reference recording with the audio rendered from a candidate score using two established\n270\ttime-series alignment distances: dynamic time warping (DTW) (Sakoe and Chiba, 1978) and time warp edit\n271\tdistance (TWED) (Marteau, 2009). We use these metrics\n272\tbecause score renderings and real performances may differ in local tempo, timing, and duration even when they\n273\tpreserve similar musical content. Alignment-based distances therefore provide a more flexible comparison than\n274\tframewise distances computed at fixed time indices.\n275\tDTW finds a minimum-cost alignment path between\n276\tthe reference and rendered feature sequences. We evaluate each of the 14 feature representations separately.\n277\tFor a given representation, each frame is represented\n278\tby a feature-specific multidimensional vector, such as\n279\tpitch-class bins, spectral coefficients, or multiband onsetstrength values. We use cosine distance between the corresponding reference and candidate frame vectors as the\n280\tlocal alignment cost and normalize the accumulated cost\n281\tby the length of the warping path. Lower DTW cost indicates greater similarity between the two feature trajectories.\n282\t\n283\t\f5\n284\t\n285\tCompared with DTW, TWED explicitly penalizes temporal gaps and edit operations, making it a stricter alignment metric that can reduce over-alignment. TWED\n286\tis parameterized by scalars 𝜈, which controls temporal stiffness, and 𝜆, which controls the edit/deletion penalty. We evaluate four settings, (𝜈, 𝜆) ∈\n287\t{(0.001, 1.0), (0.01, 1.0), (0.001, 2.0), (0.01, 2.0)}.\n288\tBecause DTW and TWED are distances (lower is better),\n289\twe negate their system-level costs when computing rank\n290\tcorrelations against higher-is-better human and modelbased scores. Full definitions, local costs, and normalization are given in Supplementary File, Appendix A.5.\n291\t\n292\t2.2.4 Model-based and embedding playback metrics\n293\tBeyond DSP feature alignment, we consider three families of learned playback-side evaluators, defined here\n294\tand validated against the human listening study in §4.4:\n295\tpairwise AudioLM judges, CLaMP 3 embeddings, and\n296\tCLEWS embeddings. Unlike DTW and TWED, these are\n297\tmodel-based and can shift with model version, prompt,\n298\tembedding preprocessing, or API availability, a tradeoff\n299\twe quantify in §4.5.\n300\tAudioLM evaluation uses the same referenceanchored format as the human task (explained more in\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "5542e233-7c79-423c-9368-82328d18ff7c", "uuid": "63fdf0ed-6556-40b5-90cc-1a136c8b27c0", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T05:58:35.789Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608_04511.txt", "content": "A Dual Evaluation for\nMusic Transcription\nRESEARCH ARTICLE\nPING WANG\n\narXiv:2608.04511v1 [cs.SD] 5 Aug 2026\n\nGUANG YANG\nNAZIF CAN TAMER\nVICTORIA EBERT\nNOAH A. SMITH\n*Author affiliations can be found in the back matter of this article\n\nABSTRACT\nAutomatic music transcription systems produce sheet music that can be read and\nplayed back. We argue that these two targets call for complementary evaluations of\nnotation similarity to a reference score and playback similarity to the original performance, respectively. Our study considers notation similarity metrics from the optical music recognition literature and a wide range of playback-similarity methods validated through a listening study across over 100 participants and 230 piano recordings covering 23 works, 30 performers, and six composers. We find, fortuitously, that\nthe playback similarity metric that correlates best with human judgments, CLEWS, is\nalso the cheapest to run. We also find that the two evaluation dimensions favor different systems among a collection of 24 pipelines formed by pairing eight audio-toMIDI models with three MIDI-to-score converters, with the latter component systematically determining the favored objective. The complementarity between metrics also\nholds when adding to the pool Rubato, a new end-to-end system that offers substantially improved notation similarity while remaining competitive, though not the best,\non playback similarity.\n\nKEYWORDS:\nautomatic music transcription,\ndual evaluation, evaluation\nmethodology, human\nlistening study, notation\nsimilarity, playback similarity\n\n\f2\n\n1 INTRODUCTION\nMusic is primarily experienced through two principal\nmodalities: sight and sound. For many musical genres, engraved musical notation (sheet music) communicates performance instructions through spatial layout and notation structure. When performers play back\nthe sheet music, they render the symbolic notation into\nsound experienced by the listener. Importantly, no two\nmusicians will play the same sheet music the same way.\nDifferences in tempo, dynamics, and timing will be audible but not visible in conventional visual notation. If a\npiece of sheet music were always played identically by\nevery performer, there would be no need to ever record\nversions other than the original. If sheet music encoded\nevery difference between performances, it would likely be\nunreadable by musicians.\nIn this work, we evaluate the tradeoff in notational and\nplayback fidelity of 25 complete automatic music transcription (AMT) systems — 24 modular pipelines and one\nend-to-end model — and highlight the importance of a\nmulti-view evaluation of AMT.\nAMT has been decomposed into subtasks, including\nbeat tracking (Foscarin et al., 2024), audio-to-MIDI note\ndetection (Bittner et al., 2022; Bradshaw et al., 2024;\nGardner et al., 2022; Hawthorne et al., 2021; Kong et al.,\n2021; Simon et al., 2022; Yan et al., 2021; Yan and Duan,\n2024), and MIDI-to-score conversion (Beyer and Dai,\n2024; Cuthbert and Ariza, 2010; MuseScore, 2021). These\nsubtasks emphasize different parts of the audio-to-score\npipeline: audio-to-MIDI systems focus on recovering performed musical events from sound, while MIDI-to-score\nsystems focus on converting symbolic events into readable notation. However, evaluating a complete audio-toscore system, as opposed to a component, requires considering both how the output appears as a written musical document and how it sounds when rendered back to\naudio.\nWe therefore formulate audio-to-score transcription\nevaluation as a two-dimensional problem. The first\ndimension seeks notation similarity: the generated sheet\nmusic should closely match the reference sheet music\nas a written musical document. The second dimension\nseeks playback similarity: audio rendered from the generated sheet music should faithfully reproduce the music\nin the original performance recording. These dimensions\nare complementary: transcribed sheet music with high\nnotation similarity may still have low playback similarity when rendered (e.g., because of an expressive performance), while a transcription with high playback similarity may offer a symbolic representation with low notation\nsimilarity or poor readability. The examples in Figure 1\nillustrate this tension.\nTo study this tradeoff between notational similarity and playback similarity, we evaluate 24 complete audio-to-score transcription pipelines formed by\n\ncombining eight competitive and widely used audioto-MIDI models with three MIDI-to-score converters.\nThese components include recent transformer-based\nsystems, piano-specific transcription models, lightweight\ntranscription baselines, and commonly used notationconversion tools, giving broad coverage of strongest publicly available audio-to-score pipelines. For notation similarity, we compare generated MusicXML scores against\nreference scores using OMR Normalized Edit Distance\n(OMR-NED; Martinez-Sevilla et al., 2025), a normalized\nedit-distance metric designed for symbolic music notation comparison. For playback similarity, we render each\ngenerated score back to audio and compare it with the\noriginal recording. This playback-side evaluation includes\ndigital signal processing (DSP)-based feature-alignment\nmetrics, dynamic time warping (DTW) (Sakoe and Chiba,\n1978) and time warp edit distance (TWED) (Marteau,\n2009), applied to multiple frame-level audio representations spanning pitch-class and harmonic, spectral, and\nrhythm and onset information, thereby capturing complementary aspects of pitch, harmony, timbre, onset\nactivity, tempo, and temporal structure; human ABX\nlistening judgments; pairwise AudioLM judges; and\nlearned embedding-similarity evaluators.\nBy considering notational fidelity as a separate dimension from playback fidelity, we cast light on the practical tradeoff between them: different transcription systems, and the developers who build them, may reasonably choose to optimize for different uses, from producing clean, musician-readable scores to capturing the\ndetails needed for faithful audio resynthesis or performance analysis.\nOur contributions are:\n• We formulate audio-to-score transcription evaluation\nas a two-dimensional problem seeking both notation\nand playback similarity.\n• We evaluate 24 complete audio-to-score pipelines\nthat produce MusicXML scores from audio recordings,\nseparating the effects of eight audio-to-MIDI models\nand three MIDI-to-score converters.\n• We collect a human ABX listening reference with 106\nparticipants and 3,180 pairwise judgments, and use it\nto validate DTW, TWED, and AudioLM judges, and\nlearned embedding evaluators, including CLaMP 3\nand CLEWS.\n• We show that notation-side and playback-side\nevaluation can prefer different converter families,\nmotivating multi-view evaluation and tradeoffs in\nsystem design.\n• We evaluate a contemporaneously developed\nend-to-end transcription system, Rubato (Tamer\net al., 2026) in the same framework.\n\n\f3\n\nFigure 1 Tradeoff between notation and playback similarity in audio-to-score transcription. The top row is a four-measure reference\nexcerpt from Beethoven, Piano Sonata No. 6, third movement, Op. 10 No. 2. Candidate A is produced by Aria-AMT (Bradshaw et al.,\n2024) + MIDI2ScoreTransformer (Beyer and Dai, 2024) and Candidate B by ismir2021 (Hawthorne et al., 2021) +\nMuseScore (MuseScore, 2021). Candidate A is more similar to the reference as written notation (OMR-NED = 49.3) but less similar in\nrendered playback (TWED cost = 1.260); Candidate B is the opposite (OMR-NED = 98.1, TWED cost = 0.322). Lower OMR-NED and\nlower TWED are better. Notation-side and playback-side evaluation favor different outputs.\n\n2 METHODOLOGY\nOur proposed evaluation setup distinguishes two primary\ndimensions: notation similarity (§2.1) and playback similarity (§2.2).\n\nLet Ŷ denote a transcription and Y the reference sheet\n̂ Y) and D(Y,\n̂ Y) be the numbers of symmusic, and let I(Y,\nbol insertions and deletions in the cheapest transformation of Ŷ into Y. The per-score OMR-NED is\n̂ Y) =\nOMR-NED(Y,\n\n2.1 NOTATION SIMILARITY\nOur notation-side evaluation treats the generated sheet\nmusic as a visual, written musical document. We use\nOMR Normalized Edit Distance (OMR-NED) (MartinezSevilla et al., 2025), a metric developed for optical\nmusic recognition (OMR) evaluation. OMR-NED compares\nexplicit notation objects in the predicted and reference\nsheet music. Although its input here is a symbolic score\nrather than a page image, its target is the visual notation\ndomain: it asks whether the symbols that would appear\non the page match the reference notation. OMR-NED is\nhence the best available operationalization of the kind of\nnotation similarity sought by AMT; see §2.1.1 for a discussion of alternatives.\nOMR-NED defines edit operations over music-notation\nsymbols: note and rest properties such as pitch, accidentals, note heads, beams and ties, and non-note objects\nsuch as clefs, key and time signatures, dynamics, slurs\nand lyrics. It therefore measures differences in the sheet\nmusic that would require manual correction by a user of\nan OMR (or AMT) system.\n\n̂ Y) + D(Y,\n̂ Y)\nI(Y,\n,\n̂ + |Y|\n|Y|\n\n1\n\nwhere | ⋅ | counts notation symbols, so the metric is\nlength-aware. Lower is better and zero is an exact match.\nFollowing Martinez-Sevilla et al. (2025), we report the\nmicro-average over evaluated files,\n∑\nOverall OMR-NED =\n\nk\n\n∑\nI(Ŷ k , Yk ) + k D(Ŷ k , Yk )\n,\n∑ ̂\n∑\n|Yk | + k |Yk |\nk\n\n2\n\nwhere k indexes evaluated recording and sheet-music\npairs. For readability, all reported OMR-NED values, in both\ntables and figures, are scaled by 100. The full symbol\ninventory and the treatment of substitutions are given in\nSupplementary File, Appendix A.1.\n\n2.1.1 Related work: AMT evaluation\nMost automatic music transcription evaluation is\nreference-based: a system output is compared with a\nground-truth representation, and the metric defines\nwhich differences matter. For audio-to-MIDI transcription, this comparison is usually made over frame-level\npitch activations or note events, using precision, recall,\nand F-measure over estimated pitches, onsets, and\noffsets (Raffel et al., 2014). These metrics are appropriate when the target output is MIDI-like note events.\nHowever, for transcription that produces sheet music as\nits final output, evaluation must consider whether the\n\n\f4\n\nresulting notation is musically interpretable and visually\nusable.\nThree notation-oriented metrics are the closest alternatives to OMR-NED. Cogliati and Duan (2017) count\nnotation errors across dimensions such as notes, durations, rests, barlines and staff assignment, and map them\nto human ratings with a regression fitted on a small rated\nset; the counts are not disjoint, so one structural mistake\ncan be penalised several times. MV2H (McLeod, 2019;\nMcLeod and Steedman, 2018) enforces disjoint penalties over five dimensions, but targets musical structure\nrather than engraving, and its MusicXML workflow compares MIDI-derived events rather than notation objects.\nMUSTER (Hiramatsu et al., 2021; Nakamura et al., 2018)\nreports separate error rates, of which only five noteoriented ones enter its MeanER.\nWe applied MUSTER to the same sheet music. Its subscores do not support a consistent interpretation (Supplementary File, Table A.1): the structure-oriented VoiceER,\nHandER and ScaleErr track OMR-NED (𝜌 = 0.94, 0.81,\n0.81), whereas MeanER and OnsetER track human playback preference far more closely (𝜌 = 0.79 and 0.84) than\nOMR-NED (𝜌 = 0.38 and 0.50). Choosing a different subscore therefore changes the conclusion, while MeanER\nomits score-organization properties central to the visual\ntask. We use OMR-NED for the main notation comparison;\nfull MUSTER scores and ranks are in Supplementary File,\nTables A.4 and A.5, and the three metrics are discussed\nat length in Supplementary File, Appendix A.3.\n\nlearned bidirectional model; we therefore do not expect\nthat using MuseScore’s rendering abilities in this way\ngives any advantage to evaluated pipelines that use MuseScore for MIDI-to-score conversion. The rendered audio\nis produced at 44.1 kHz and peak-normalized.\n\n2.2.2 Audio feature representations\nPlayback similarity is computed over frame-level\naudio features. Because transcription quality is\nmultidimensional—pitch, timing, rhythm, duration, and\nharmony—and no single feature captures all of it, we\nevaluate fourteen features (Bogdanov et al., 2013; McFee\net al., 2015) in three groups: pitch-class and harmonic\n(Chroma, HPCP, Chroma CENS, Tonnetz, CQT, STFT semitone, pitch salience), capturing note content, harmonic\nstructure and tonal motion; spectral (Mel spectrogram,\nMFCC, PCEN-Mel, Log-STFT, spectral flux), which can be\nsensitive to rendering artifacts; and rhythm/onset (onset\nstrength, tempogram). Each feature is listed with its\ncitation and musical property in Supplementary File,\nTable A.7.\nAll reference and rendered audio is processed at\n44.1 kHz. Frame-based features use a 10 ms hop with\nframe-wise normalization before distances are computed, and tempogram is derived from onset strength.\nEach representation is scored independently: per feature\nwe compute one reference–candidate DTW or TWED distance per recording and average over the evaluation set\nto obtain one system-level score. The per-feature scores\nare compared in §4.4.\n\n2.2 PLAYBACK SIMILARITY\nWe treat playback similarity as the second evaluation\ndimension measured through multiple evaluator families. We first render each transcribed score to audio\n(§2.2.1) and extract frame-level audio representations\nfrom the original and rendered recordings (§2.2.2).\nWe then compare the recordings using deterministic\nDSP-based feature-alignment metrics, DTW and TWED\n(§2.2.3), and learned or model-based evaluators, including AudioLM judges, CLaMP 3, and CLEWS (§2.2.4). We\nvalidate these automatic evaluators against human pairwise listening judgments in §4.4 and compare their practical costs in §4.5.\n\n2.2.1 Audio rendering\nAudio-based evaluation requires converting symbolic\nrepresentation into audio. Therefore, for each system, we\nconvert the predicted MusicXML score to MIDI and then\nto waveform audio. We use the same rendering setup\nacross systems.\nThe rendering pipeline uses MuseScore to export\nMusicXML files to MIDI, followed by FluidSynth (FluidSynth\nContributors, 2022) to synthesize WAV audio using the\nSGM soundfont (Shan, 2024). MuseScore is used here only\nas a fixed downstream renderer, and its relevant import/export routines are rule-based rather than a shared\n\n2.2.3 Feature-alignment playback metrics\nAfter extracting frame-level audio features, we compare each reference recording with the audio rendered from a candidate score using two established\ntime-series alignment distances: dynamic time warping (DTW) (Sakoe and Chiba, 1978) and time warp edit\ndistance (TWED) (Marteau, 2009). We use these metrics\nbecause score renderings and real performances may differ in local tempo, timing, and duration even when they\npreserve similar musical content. Alignment-based distances therefore provide a more flexible comparison than\nframewise distances computed at fixed time indices.\nDTW finds a minimum-cost alignment path between\nthe reference and rendered feature sequences. We evaluate each of the 14 feature representations separately.\nFor a given representation, each frame is represented\nby a feature-specific multidimensional vector, such as\npitch-class bins, spectral coefficients, or multiband onsetstrength values. We use cosine distance between the corresponding reference and candidate frame vectors as the\nlocal alignment cost and normalize the accumulated cost\nby the length of the warping path. Lower DTW cost indicates greater similarity between the two feature trajectories.\n\n\f5\n\nCompared with DTW, TWED explicitly penalizes temporal gaps and edit operations, making it a stricter alignment metric that can reduce over-alignment. TWED\nis parameterized by scalars 𝜈, which controls temporal stiffness, and 𝜆, which controls the edit/deletion penalty. We evaluate four settings, (𝜈, 𝜆) ∈\n{(0.001, 1.0), (0.01, 1.0), (0.001, 2.0), (0.01, 2.0)}.\nBecause DTW and TWED are distances (lower is better),\nwe negate their system-level costs when computing rank\ncorrelations against higher-is-better human and modelbased scores. Full definitions, local costs, and normalization are given in Supplementary File, Appendix A.5.\n\n2.2.4 Model-based and embedding playback metrics\nBeyond DSP feature alignment, we consider three families of learned playback-side evaluators, defined here\nand validated against the human listening study in §4.4:\npairwise AudioLM judges, CLaMP 3 embeddings, and\nCLEWS embeddings. Unlike DTW and TWED, these are\nmodel-based and can shift with model version, prompt,\nembedding preprocessing, or API availability, a tradeoff\nwe quantify in §4.5.\nAudioLM evaluation uses the same referenceanchored format as the human task (explained more in", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 3822}}}
