{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a5e585aad4495082", "type": "tool_result", "content": "700\t,\n701\texp(𝜃i ) + exp(𝜃j )\n702\t\n703\t4\n704\t\n705\twhere  is the multiset of observed winner–loser pairs\n706\t(i, j). We impose the standard identifiability constraint\n707\t∑\n708\t𝜃 = 0. The resulting maximum-likelihood estii i\n709\tmate gives one human Bradley–Terry score per pipeline;\n710\t\n711\tlarger 𝜃i indicates a stronger human preference. The raw\n712\tBradley–Terry scores are shown in Supplementary File,\n713\tTable A.2.\n714\t\n715\t4.4.3 DSP-based feature-alignment metric validation\n716\tWe use Spearman correlation (Spearman, 1904) with\n717\tBradley–Terry scores as the primary selection criterion\n718\tbecause it measures monotonic agreement between\n719\tsystem-level rankings, and report Kendall’s 𝜏 (Kendall,\n720\t1938) as a secondary pairwise-agreement check. DTW\n721\tand TWED costs are negated before correlation so higher\n722\tis better.\n723\tDTW and TWED are deterministic DSP featurealignment distances: they learn no parameters from our\n724\tbenchmark, but their behavior depends on the input\n725\tfeature representation. Applying each metric to each\n726\tfeature (one at a time), the best DTW setting is Chroma\n727\tCENS (Spearman 𝜌 = 0.891, Kendall 𝜏 = 0.761), and\n728\tthe best TWED setting is MFCC with (𝜈, 𝜆) = (0.001, 2.0)\n729\t(𝜌 = 0.924, 𝜏 = 0.790). Table 1 summarizes the strongest\n730\tevaluators; the full TWED sweep is in Supplementary File,\n731\tAppendix A.8 and the full per-feature DTW/TWED results\n732\tin Appendix A.9.\n733\t\n734\t4.4.4 Model-based and embedding-based evaluation validation\n735\tThe model-based and embedding-based results in\n736\tTable 1 compare the evaluators defined in §2.2.4 against\n737\tthe same Bradley–Terry scores. CLEWS segment-mean\n738\tsimilarity shows the strongest agreement with human\n739\tpreference (Spearman 𝜌 = 0.971, Kendall 𝜏 = 0.891), narrowly followed by the position-balanced Gemini 3.1 Pro\n740\tjudge (𝜌 = 0.970, 𝜏 = 0.870). CLaMP 3 audio segmentmedian similarity also shows strong agreement (𝜌 =\n741\t0.884, 𝜏 = 0.710), a little below DTW. Full model- and\n742\tvariant-level results are reported in Supplementary File,\n743\tAppendix A.9.\n744\tAlthough CLaMP 3 ABC does not compare rendered\n745\taudio, we also evaluate its agreement with human playback preference to examine the effect of CLaMP 3’s\n746\tshared multimodal representation. Its global cosine score\n747\tcorrelates more strongly with human preference (Spearman 𝜌 = 0.824, Kendall 𝜏 = 0.659) than with OMR-NED\n748\t(𝜌 = 0.681, 𝜏 = 0.478). This suggests that the shared\n749\tembedding emphasizes modality-invariant musical content even when its input is symbolic. We therefore interpret CLaMP 3 ABC as a measure of broad musical correspondence, not as an explicit notation or playback metric.\n750\t\n751\t4.5 COST ANALYSIS\n752\tAgreement with human preference is only one criterion\n753\tfor selecting an evaluator; cost, reproducibility, and coverage also matter. Local notation, DSP, and embedding\n754\tmetrics score all reference–candidate pairs, whereas the\n755\t\n756\t\f10\n757\t\n758\tEvaluator\n759\t\n760\tRepresentation / input\n761\t\n762\tSpearman 𝜌 ↑\n763\t\n764\tKendall 𝜏 ↑\n765\t\n766\tCLEWS\n767\t\n768\tsegment-mean audio embedding\n769\t\n770\t0.971\n771\t\n772\t0.891\n773\t\n774\tGemini 3.1 Pro\n775\t\n776\tAudio ABX prompt\n777\t\n778\t0.970\n779\t\n780\t0.870\n781\t\n782\tTWED\n783\t\n784\tMFCC; selected cost negated\n785\t\n786\t0.924\n787\t\n788\t0.790\n789\t\n790\tDTW\n791\t\n792\tChroma CENS; cost negated\n793\t\n794\t0.891\n795\t\n796\t0.761\n797\t\n798\tCLaMP 3 audio\n799\t\n800\tsegment-median audio embedding\n801\t\n802\t0.884\n803\t\n804\t0.710\n805\t\n806\tCLaMP 3 audio→ABC\n807\t\n808\tmedian ref-audio/pred-ABC cosine\n809\t\n810\t0.879\n811\t\n812\t0.710\n813\t\n814\tCLaMP 3 ABC→audio\n815\t\n816\tmean ref-ABC/pred-audio cosine\n817\t\n818\t0.849\n819\t\n820\t0.717\n821\t\n822\tTable 1 Automatic playback-side evaluators ranked by rank correlation with Bradley–Terry scores. For DTW and TWED we report the\n823\tstrongest feature (Chroma CENS and MFCC, respectively). Higher correlation is better.\n824\tEvaluator\n825\t\n826\tUnit\n827\t\n828\tNum. units\n829\t\n830\tCost estimate\n831\t\n832\tNormalized cost\n833\t\n834\tOMR-NED\n835\t\n836\tsymbolic-score pair\n837\t\n838\t5,520\n839\t\n840\t$1.10\n841\t\n842\t$0.200 / 1,000 units\n843\t\n844\tDTW\n845\t\n846\taudio-pair score\n847\t\n848\t5,520\n849\t\n850\t$3.37\n851\t\n852\t$0.610 / 1,000 units\n853\t\n854\tTWED–MFCC\n855\t\n856\taudio-pair score\n857\t\n858\t5,520\n859\t\n860\t$9.74\n861\t\n862\t$1.760 / 1,000 units\n863\t\n864\tCLaMP 3 audio\n865\t\n866\taudio-pair score\n867\t\n868\t5,520\n869\t\n870\t$15.45\n871\t\n872\t$2.800 / 1,000 units\n873\t\n874\tCLaMP 3 audio/ABC\n875\t\n876\taudio–symbolic pair\n877\t\n878\t5,424\n879\t\n880\t$3.99\n881\t\n882\t$0.740 / 1,000 units\n883\t\n884\tCLaMP 3 ABC\n885\t\n886\tsymbolic-score pair\n887\t\n888\t5,261\n889\t\n890\t$0.95\n891\t\n892\t$0.180 / 1,000 units\n893\t\n894\tCLEWS\n895\t\n896\taudio-pair score\n897\t\n898\t5,520\n899\t\n900\t$2.06\n901\t\n902\t$0.370 / 1,000 units\n903\t\n904\tGemini 3.1 Pro\n905\t\n906\tABX request (both orders)\n907\t\n908\t3,600\n909\t\n910\t$18.36\n911\t\n912\t$5.100 / 1,000 units\n913\t\n914\tHuman ABX\n915\t\n916\tjudgment\n917\t\n918\t3,180\n919\t\n920\t$1,060.00\n921\t\n922\t$333.333 / 1,000 units\n923\t\n924\tTable 2 Evaluation cost across notation, feature-alignment, learned-embedding, AudioLM, and human evaluation. Dollar amounts for\n925\tlocal metrics are cloud-equivalent estimates; AudioLM costs are paid-API estimates; human ABX is participant compensation. Normalized values are useful within this benchmark but are not exact like-for-like comparisons across evaluator families.\n926\t\n927\thuman and AudioLM studies use sampled pairwise comparisons. Table 2 therefore reports each method’s cost\n928\tper 1,000 comparison units.\n929\tFigure 5 makes the practical tradeoff explicit. CLEWS\n930\tprovides the strongest cost–agreement balance, combining the highest human correlation with the lowest\n931\tnormalized cost among the five playback evaluators.\n932\tDTW remains attractive when a DSP, model-independent\n933\tscreen is preferred. TWED increases agreement relative to\n934\tDTW at higher compute cost, while Gemini approaches\n935\tCLEWS’s agreement at the highest automatic-evaluation\n936\tcost shown. CLaMP 3 audio provides another learned\n937\trepresentation view, but does not improve the cost–\n938\tagreement frontier here.\n939\t\n940\t5 DISCUSSION\n941\tFor AMT research, we recommend reporting and comparing measures of both notation similarity and playback similarity. For reasons discussed in §2.1.1, we recommend OMR-NED for the former, at this writing. On the\n942\tplayback side, CLEWS (segment-mean) is the strongest\n943\tchoice among the automatic playback similarity evaluators we considered, in terms of both correlation to human\n944\tpreferences and cost (Figure 5). If a stable, model-free\n945\toption is required, TWED–MFCC or DTW–Chroma CENS\n946\tshould be considered, depending on budget. We do not\n947\trecommend AudioLM judges at this writing; while they\n948\tcan provide ABX-format preference comparisons that\n949\t\n950\tapproximate human judgments almost as well as CLEWS,\n951\tthey are ∼13× as expensive and depend on model version, prompt, and parsing choices (as well as proprietary and unstable model weights, in many cases). Our\n952\tmethodology in §4.4 can be applied to assess new proposals for automatic playback similarity evaluation.\n953\t\n954\t6 LIMITATIONS\n955\tThis study focuses on Western classical piano recordings,\n956\tevaluated over the first three minutes of each recording; conclusions may not transfer to other instruments,\n957\tensembles, voice, non-classical genres, or longer structures. Playback-side results depend on the rendering\n958\tpipeline: although all systems are rendered consistently,\n959\tthe renderer, soundfont, and normalization could affect\n960\tboth automatic metrics and listeners. We also do not validate notation quality against human score-reading judgments; OMR-NED measures symbol similarity but may\n961\tnot reflect the effort to read or edit generated sheet\n962\tmusic. Finally, our human-validated comparison targets\n963\tpipelined systems; we take a first step toward end-toend audio-to-score models with the Rubato case study\n964\t(§4.3), but Rubato postdates the listening study and so\n965\tlacks human judgments, leaving its perceived playback\n966\tquality an open question. Future work might add human\n967\tvisual evaluation of readability and test the stability of\n968\tmetric selection across held-out pieces, participants, and\n969\tsystems.\n970\t\n971\t\f11\n972\t\n973\tSpearman ρ with human\n974\t\n975\t0.98\n976\t\n977\tCLEWS\n978\t\n979\tGemini 3.1 Pro\n980\t\n981\t0.96\n982\t\n983\tEvaluator family\n984\tDSP\n985\tLearned embedding\n986\tAudioLM judge\n987\t\n988\t0.94\n989\tTWED–MFCC\n990\t0.92\n991\t\n992\t0.90\n993\t\n994\t0.88\n995\t\n996\tDTW–Chroma CENS\n997\t\n998\tCLaMP 3 audio\n999\t5\n1000\t\n1001\t2\n1002\t\n1003\t1\n1004\t\n1005\t0.5\n1006\t\n1007\tNormalized cost per 1,000 units (cheaper → )\n1008\tFigure 5 Cost–human agreement tradeoff for five automatic playback similarity evaluators. Cost is normalized per 1,000 evaluation\n1009\tunits using Table 2; the horizontal axis is reversed so that cost decreases to the right, and the vertical axis reports Spearman correlation\n1010\twith human-derived Bradley–Terry scores using Table 1. Better evaluators are therefore up and to the right, as in the other figures.\n1011\t\n1012\t7 CONCLUSION\n1013\tWe presented a dual evaluation of audio-to-score transcription that separates written-score similarity from\n1014\trendered-playback similarity. These two views rank systems differently because they measure distinct properties of the output. In general, we recommend that music\n1015\ttranscription evaluations include methods to assess both\n1016\tthe extent to which the generated sheet music resembles the reference and the extent to which it preserves the\n1017\tmusical content of the original recording when rendered,\n1018\tand future transcription systems intended for diverse use\n1019\tshould consider test-time controls that allow a user to\n1020\ttrade off between these two different goals.\n1021\t\n1022\tFUNDING INFORMATION\n1023\tParticipant compensation, reported in Table 2, was covered by the last author’s discretionary research funding.\n1024\t\n1025\tCOMPETING INTERESTS\n1026\tThe authors have no competing interests to declare.\n1027\t\n1028\tAUTHORS’ CONTRIBUTIONS\n1029\tPW designed and implemented the evaluation pipeline,\n1030\tran the transcription, rendering and metric computations, designed and deployed the human listening\n1031\tstudy, analysed the results, and led the writing of the\n1032\tmanuscript. GY contributed to the OMR-NED experiments,\n1033\tadvised on OMR-NED and the notation-side evaluation,\n1034\tand provided ongoing methodological guidance throughout the project. NCT advised on the Rubato case study\n1035\t\n1036\tand on the CLaMP 3 and CLEWS embedding evaluators.\n1037\tVE advised on the manuscript and its figures and on the\n1038\tanalysis of the results. NAS conceived the project, supervised it, and served as principal investigator on the IRB\n1039\tprotocol. All authors contributed to the framing and writing of the paper.\n1040\t\n1041\tUSE OF GENERATIVE AI\n1042\tGenerative AI tools were used to check grammar and\n1043\twording, and to assist with writing parts of the evaluation and analysis code, which the authors reviewed and\n1044\ttested. They were not used to generate research ideas,\n1045\tdesign the evaluation, produce or interpret any result,\n1046\tor write the scientific content. Audio language models were separately evaluated as candidate playbacksimilarity metrics, as described in §2.2.4.\n1047\t\n1048\tETHICS AND CONSENT\n1049\tThis study included a human listening experiment\n1050\t(§4.4.1) with 106 participants aged 18 or over, conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki.\n1051\tThe protocol, ‘Human Evaluation of Automatic Music\n1052\tTranscription Systems’, was reviewed by the University\n1053\tof Washington Human Subjects Division, which on 12\n1054\tMarch 2026 determined it to be human subjects research\n1055\tqualifying for exempt status under Category 2 (IRB ID\n1056\tSTUDY00025192). Each participant gave informed consent through an electronic form stating the purpose, procedure, duration, risks, benefits, compensation and voluntary nature of the study, and could stop at any time\n1057\twithout penalty; documentation of written consent was\n1058\t\n1059\t\f12\n1060\t\n1061\twaived for this anonymous, minimal-risk online experiment. No directly identifying information was collected,\n1062\tand compensation was a gift certificate requiring none.\n1063\t\n1064\tREPRODUCIBILITY\n1065\tThe evaluation code, the de-identified human ABX\n1066\tresponse data and the per-system scores are released at\n1067\thttps://github.com/pingw220/AMT-Dual-Eval under the\n1068\tMIT License, with the released data tables under\n1069\tCC BY 4.0. The release covers the rendered-audio\n1070\t(DTW/TWED) dimension and the human listening study;\n1071\tit excludes reference and rendered audio, MusicXML and\n1072\tMIDI outputs, and third-party model outputs, which\n1073\tremain subject to their own licences. Participant identifiers are replaced with surrogate ids and the mapping\n1074\tis not released; demographics appear only as aggregate\n1075\tcounts.\n1076\t\n1077\tAUTHOR AFFILIATIONS\n1078\tPing Wang\n1079\tUniversity of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA,\n1080\tpingw220@cs.washington.edu\n1081\tGuang Yang\n1082\tUniversity of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA,\n1083\tgyang1@cs.washington.edu\n1084\tNazif Can Tamer\n1085\tUniversity of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA,\n1086\tnctamer@cs.washington.edu\n1087\tVictoria Ebert\n1088\tUniversity of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA,\n1089\tebertv@cs.washington.edu\n1090\tNoah A. Smith\n1091\tUniversity of Washington and Allen Institute for Artificial\n1092\tIntelligence, Seattle, WA, USA, nasmith@cs.washington.edu\n1093\t\n1094\t\f13\n1095\t\n1096\tREFERENCES\n1097\t\n1098\tEmiya, V., Bertin, N., David, B., and Badeau, R. (2010). MAPS: A\n1099\tpiano database for multipitch estimation and automatic\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "5542e233-7c79-423c-9368-82328d18ff7c", "uuid": "532e97f4-f8df-4499-a938-b4b21e5b8585", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T05:59:58.809Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608_04511.txt", "content": ",\nexp(𝜃i ) + exp(𝜃j )\n\n4\n\nwhere  is the multiset of observed winner–loser pairs\n(i, j). We impose the standard identifiability constraint\n∑\n𝜃 = 0. The resulting maximum-likelihood estii i\nmate gives one human Bradley–Terry score per pipeline;\n\nlarger 𝜃i indicates a stronger human preference. The raw\nBradley–Terry scores are shown in Supplementary File,\nTable A.2.\n\n4.4.3 DSP-based feature-alignment metric validation\nWe use Spearman correlation (Spearman, 1904) with\nBradley–Terry scores as the primary selection criterion\nbecause it measures monotonic agreement between\nsystem-level rankings, and report Kendall’s 𝜏 (Kendall,\n1938) as a secondary pairwise-agreement check. DTW\nand TWED costs are negated before correlation so higher\nis better.\nDTW and TWED are deterministic DSP featurealignment distances: they learn no parameters from our\nbenchmark, but their behavior depends on the input\nfeature representation. Applying each metric to each\nfeature (one at a time), the best DTW setting is Chroma\nCENS (Spearman 𝜌 = 0.891, Kendall 𝜏 = 0.761), and\nthe best TWED setting is MFCC with (𝜈, 𝜆) = (0.001, 2.0)\n(𝜌 = 0.924, 𝜏 = 0.790). Table 1 summarizes the strongest\nevaluators; the full TWED sweep is in Supplementary File,\nAppendix A.8 and the full per-feature DTW/TWED results\nin Appendix A.9.\n\n4.4.4 Model-based and embedding-based evaluation validation\nThe model-based and embedding-based results in\nTable 1 compare the evaluators defined in §2.2.4 against\nthe same Bradley–Terry scores. CLEWS segment-mean\nsimilarity shows the strongest agreement with human\npreference (Spearman 𝜌 = 0.971, Kendall 𝜏 = 0.891), narrowly followed by the position-balanced Gemini 3.1 Pro\njudge (𝜌 = 0.970, 𝜏 = 0.870). CLaMP 3 audio segmentmedian similarity also shows strong agreement (𝜌 =\n0.884, 𝜏 = 0.710), a little below DTW. Full model- and\nvariant-level results are reported in Supplementary File,\nAppendix A.9.\nAlthough CLaMP 3 ABC does not compare rendered\naudio, we also evaluate its agreement with human playback preference to examine the effect of CLaMP 3’s\nshared multimodal representation. Its global cosine score\ncorrelates more strongly with human preference (Spearman 𝜌 = 0.824, Kendall 𝜏 = 0.659) than with OMR-NED\n(𝜌 = 0.681, 𝜏 = 0.478). This suggests that the shared\nembedding emphasizes modality-invariant musical content even when its input is symbolic. We therefore interpret CLaMP 3 ABC as a measure of broad musical correspondence, not as an explicit notation or playback metric.\n\n4.5 COST ANALYSIS\nAgreement with human preference is only one criterion\nfor selecting an evaluator; cost, reproducibility, and coverage also matter. Local notation, DSP, and embedding\nmetrics score all reference–candidate pairs, whereas the\n\n\f10\n\nEvaluator\n\nRepresentation / input\n\nSpearman 𝜌 ↑\n\nKendall 𝜏 ↑\n\nCLEWS\n\nsegment-mean audio embedding\n\n0.971\n\n0.891\n\nGemini 3.1 Pro\n\nAudio ABX prompt\n\n0.970\n\n0.870\n\nTWED\n\nMFCC; selected cost negated\n\n0.924\n\n0.790\n\nDTW\n\nChroma CENS; cost negated\n\n0.891\n\n0.761\n\nCLaMP 3 audio\n\nsegment-median audio embedding\n\n0.884\n\n0.710\n\nCLaMP 3 audio→ABC\n\nmedian ref-audio/pred-ABC cosine\n\n0.879\n\n0.710\n\nCLaMP 3 ABC→audio\n\nmean ref-ABC/pred-audio cosine\n\n0.849\n\n0.717\n\nTable 1 Automatic playback-side evaluators ranked by rank correlation with Bradley–Terry scores. For DTW and TWED we report the\nstrongest feature (Chroma CENS and MFCC, respectively). Higher correlation is better.\nEvaluator\n\nUnit\n\nNum. units\n\nCost estimate\n\nNormalized cost\n\nOMR-NED\n\nsymbolic-score pair\n\n5,520\n\n$1.10\n\n$0.200 / 1,000 units\n\nDTW\n\naudio-pair score\n\n5,520\n\n$3.37\n\n$0.610 / 1,000 units\n\nTWED–MFCC\n\naudio-pair score\n\n5,520\n\n$9.74\n\n$1.760 / 1,000 units\n\nCLaMP 3 audio\n\naudio-pair score\n\n5,520\n\n$15.45\n\n$2.800 / 1,000 units\n\nCLaMP 3 audio/ABC\n\naudio–symbolic pair\n\n5,424\n\n$3.99\n\n$0.740 / 1,000 units\n\nCLaMP 3 ABC\n\nsymbolic-score pair\n\n5,261\n\n$0.95\n\n$0.180 / 1,000 units\n\nCLEWS\n\naudio-pair score\n\n5,520\n\n$2.06\n\n$0.370 / 1,000 units\n\nGemini 3.1 Pro\n\nABX request (both orders)\n\n3,600\n\n$18.36\n\n$5.100 / 1,000 units\n\nHuman ABX\n\njudgment\n\n3,180\n\n$1,060.00\n\n$333.333 / 1,000 units\n\nTable 2 Evaluation cost across notation, feature-alignment, learned-embedding, AudioLM, and human evaluation. Dollar amounts for\nlocal metrics are cloud-equivalent estimates; AudioLM costs are paid-API estimates; human ABX is participant compensation. Normalized values are useful within this benchmark but are not exact like-for-like comparisons across evaluator families.\n\nhuman and AudioLM studies use sampled pairwise comparisons. Table 2 therefore reports each method’s cost\nper 1,000 comparison units.\nFigure 5 makes the practical tradeoff explicit. CLEWS\nprovides the strongest cost–agreement balance, combining the highest human correlation with the lowest\nnormalized cost among the five playback evaluators.\nDTW remains attractive when a DSP, model-independent\nscreen is preferred. TWED increases agreement relative to\nDTW at higher compute cost, while Gemini approaches\nCLEWS’s agreement at the highest automatic-evaluation\ncost shown. CLaMP 3 audio provides another learned\nrepresentation view, but does not improve the cost–\nagreement frontier here.\n\n5 DISCUSSION\nFor AMT research, we recommend reporting and comparing measures of both notation similarity and playback similarity. For reasons discussed in §2.1.1, we recommend OMR-NED for the former, at this writing. On the\nplayback side, CLEWS (segment-mean) is the strongest\nchoice among the automatic playback similarity evaluators we considered, in terms of both correlation to human\npreferences and cost (Figure 5). If a stable, model-free\noption is required, TWED–MFCC or DTW–Chroma CENS\nshould be considered, depending on budget. We do not\nrecommend AudioLM judges at this writing; while they\ncan provide ABX-format preference comparisons that\n\napproximate human judgments almost as well as CLEWS,\nthey are ∼13× as expensive and depend on model version, prompt, and parsing choices (as well as proprietary and unstable model weights, in many cases). Our\nmethodology in §4.4 can be applied to assess new proposals for automatic playback similarity evaluation.\n\n6 LIMITATIONS\nThis study focuses on Western classical piano recordings,\nevaluated over the first three minutes of each recording; conclusions may not transfer to other instruments,\nensembles, voice, non-classical genres, or longer structures. Playback-side results depend on the rendering\npipeline: although all systems are rendered consistently,\nthe renderer, soundfont, and normalization could affect\nboth automatic metrics and listeners. We also do not validate notation quality against human score-reading judgments; OMR-NED measures symbol similarity but may\nnot reflect the effort to read or edit generated sheet\nmusic. Finally, our human-validated comparison targets\npipelined systems; we take a first step toward end-toend audio-to-score models with the Rubato case study\n(§4.3), but Rubato postdates the listening study and so\nlacks human judgments, leaving its perceived playback\nquality an open question. Future work might add human\nvisual evaluation of readability and test the stability of\nmetric selection across held-out pieces, participants, and\nsystems.\n\n\f11\n\nSpearman ρ with human\n\n0.98\n\nCLEWS\n\nGemini 3.1 Pro\n\n0.96\n\nEvaluator family\nDSP\nLearned embedding\nAudioLM judge\n\n0.94\nTWED–MFCC\n0.92\n\n0.90\n\n0.88\n\nDTW–Chroma CENS\n\nCLaMP 3 audio\n5\n\n2\n\n1\n\n0.5\n\nNormalized cost per 1,000 units (cheaper → )\nFigure 5 Cost–human agreement tradeoff for five automatic playback similarity evaluators. Cost is normalized per 1,000 evaluation\nunits using Table 2; the horizontal axis is reversed so that cost decreases to the right, and the vertical axis reports Spearman correlation\nwith human-derived Bradley–Terry scores using Table 1. Better evaluators are therefore up and to the right, as in the other figures.\n\n7 CONCLUSION\nWe presented a dual evaluation of audio-to-score transcription that separates written-score similarity from\nrendered-playback similarity. These two views rank systems differently because they measure distinct properties of the output. In general, we recommend that music\ntranscription evaluations include methods to assess both\nthe extent to which the generated sheet music resembles the reference and the extent to which it preserves the\nmusical content of the original recording when rendered,\nand future transcription systems intended for diverse use\nshould consider test-time controls that allow a user to\ntrade off between these two different goals.\n\nFUNDING INFORMATION\nParticipant compensation, reported in Table 2, was covered by the last author’s discretionary research funding.\n\nCOMPETING INTERESTS\nThe authors have no competing interests to declare.\n\nAUTHORS’ CONTRIBUTIONS\nPW designed and implemented the evaluation pipeline,\nran the transcription, rendering and metric computations, designed and deployed the human listening\nstudy, analysed the results, and led the writing of the\nmanuscript. GY contributed to the OMR-NED experiments,\nadvised on OMR-NED and the notation-side evaluation,\nand provided ongoing methodological guidance throughout the project. NCT advised on the Rubato case study\n\nand on the CLaMP 3 and CLEWS embedding evaluators.\nVE advised on the manuscript and its figures and on the\nanalysis of the results. NAS conceived the project, supervised it, and served as principal investigator on the IRB\nprotocol. All authors contributed to the framing and writing of the paper.\n\nUSE OF GENERATIVE AI\nGenerative AI tools were used to check grammar and\nwording, and to assist with writing parts of the evaluation and analysis code, which the authors reviewed and\ntested. They were not used to generate research ideas,\ndesign the evaluation, produce or interpret any result,\nor write the scientific content. Audio language models were separately evaluated as candidate playbacksimilarity metrics, as described in §2.2.4.\n\nETHICS AND CONSENT\nThis study included a human listening experiment\n(§4.4.1) with 106 participants aged 18 or over, conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki.\nThe protocol, ‘Human Evaluation of Automatic Music\nTranscription Systems’, was reviewed by the University\nof Washington Human Subjects Division, which on 12\nMarch 2026 determined it to be human subjects research\nqualifying for exempt status under Category 2 (IRB ID\nSTUDY00025192). Each participant gave informed consent through an electronic form stating the purpose, procedure, duration, risks, benefits, compensation and voluntary nature of the study, and could stop at any time\nwithout penalty; documentation of written consent was\n\n\f12\n\nwaived for this anonymous, minimal-risk online experiment. No directly identifying information was collected,\nand compensation was a gift certificate requiring none.\n\nREPRODUCIBILITY\nThe evaluation code, the de-identified human ABX\nresponse data and the per-system scores are released at\nhttps://github.com/pingw220/AMT-Dual-Eval under the\nMIT License, with the released data tables under\nCC BY 4.0. The release covers the rendered-audio\n(DTW/TWED) dimension and the human listening study;\nit excludes reference and rendered audio, MusicXML and\nMIDI outputs, and third-party model outputs, which\nremain subject to their own licences. Participant identifiers are replaced with surrogate ids and the mapping\nis not released; demographics appear only as aggregate\ncounts.\n\nAUTHOR AFFILIATIONS\nPing Wang\nUniversity of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA,\npingw220@cs.washington.edu\nGuang Yang\nUniversity of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA,\ngyang1@cs.washington.edu\nNazif Can Tamer\nUniversity of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA,\nnctamer@cs.washington.edu\nVictoria Ebert\nUniversity of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA,\nebertv@cs.washington.edu\nNoah A. Smith\nUniversity of Washington and Allen Institute for Artificial\nIntelligence, Seattle, WA, USA, nasmith@cs.washington.edu\n\n\f13\n\nREFERENCES\n\nEmiya, V., Bertin, N., David, B., and Badeau, R. (2010). MAPS: A\npiano database for multipitch estimation and automatic", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 700, "totalLines": 3822}}}
