{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b640f19660a7338a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1294\tYan, Y. and Duan, Z. (2024). Scoring time intervals using\n1295\tnon-hierarchical transformer for automatic piano\n1296\ttranscription. In Proc. International Society for Music\n1297\tInformation Retrieval Conference (ISMIR).\n1298\tYcart, A., Liu, L., Benetos, E., and Pearce, M. T. (2020).\n1299\tInvestigating the perceptual validity of evaluation metrics\n1300\tfor automatic piano music transcription. Transactions of\n1301\tthe International Society for Music Information Retrieval,\n1302\t3(1):68–81. 10.5334/tismir.57.\n1303\tZhang, H., Tang, J., Rafee, S. R. M., and Fazekas, S. D. G. (2022).\n1304\tAtepp: A dataset of automatically transcribed expressive\n1305\tpiano performance. In ISMIR 2022 Hybrid Conference.\n1306\t\n1307\t\f15\n1308\t\n1309\tSupplementary File: Appendix\n1310\tSupplementary Material for A Dual Evaluation for Music Transcription\n1311\t\n1312\tA.1 OMR-NED DEFINITION DETAILS\n1313\tThis section gives the full definition of OMR-NED summarised in Section 2.1 of the main paper.\n1314\tOMR-NED defines edit operations over music-notation\n1315\tsymbols. These symbols include note and rest properties, such as pitch, accidentals, note heads, beams, flags,\n1316\tdots, ties, articulations, and ornaments, as well as nonnote score objects such as clefs, key signatures, time\n1317\tsignatures, dynamics, slurs, lyrics, directions, chord symbols, and other notation elements. The metric is therefore\n1318\tintended to measure differences in the sheet music that\n1319\twould require manual correction by a user of an OMR (or,\n1320\tin our case, AMT) system.\n1321\tLet Ŷ denote a transcription and Y denote the reference sheet music. Consider a transformation from Ŷ into Y\n1322\tthrough insertions and deletions of notation symbols; let\n1323\t̂ Y) be the number of insertions and D(Y,\n1324\t̂ Y) the number\n1325\tI(Y,\n1326\tof deletions in the transformation that minimizes their\n1327\tsum. (OMR-NED does not count substitutions as a separate operation; a changed symbol is represented as one\n1328\tdeletion and one insertion.) The per-score OMR-NED is\n1329\tdefined as\n1330\t̂ Y) =\n1331\tOMR-NED(Y,\n1332\t\n1333\t̂ Y) + D(Y,\n1334\t̂ Y)\n1335\tI(Y,\n1336\t,\n1337\t̂ + |Y|\n1338\t|Y|\n1339\t\n1340\tA.1\n1341\t\n1342\twhere |⋅| denotes the number of notation-symbols in the\n1343\targument; this normalization makes OMR-NED lengthaware by scaling the edit distance by the total amount\n1344\tof notation present in both documents. Lower OMR-NED\n1345\tindicates stronger notational similarity, and a value of\n1346\tzero indicates an exact match.\n1347\tFollowing Martinez-Sevilla et al. (2025), our implementation reports a micro-averaged OMR-NED over all evaluated files:\n1348\t∑ ̂\n1349\t∑\n1350\tI(Yk , Yk ) + k D(Ŷ k , Yk )\n1351\tOverall OMR-NED = k ∑\n1352\t,\n1353\tA.2\n1354\t∑\n1355\t|Ŷ k | + k |Yk |\n1356\tk\n1357\twhere k indexes evaluated recordings and sheet-music\n1358\tpairs. For readability, all reported OMR-NED values, in both\n1359\ttables and figures, are scaled by 100.\n1360\t\n1361\tA.2 SUPPLEMENTARY SYSTEM RAW\n1362\tSCORES AND RANKINGS\n1363\tThis section reports complete 24-system raw scores\n1364\tand ranks. Table A.2 reports the raw values for the\n1365\tselected notation, rendered-audio, preference-based,\n1366\tand learned-embedding evaluators, while Table A.3\n1367\treports the corresponding rank positions. Table A.1 compares each MUSTER subscore with OMR-NED and human\n1368\t\n1369\tMUSTER score\n1370\t\n1371\tOMR-NED\n1372\t\n1373\tHuman playback\n1374\t\n1375\tMeanER\n1376\tPitchER\n1377\tMissRate\n1378\tExtraRate\n1379\tOnsetER\n1380\tOffsetER\n1381\tVoiceER\n1382\tHandER\n1383\tScaleErr\n1384\t\n1385\t0.383\n1386\t−0.345\n1387\t−0.219\n1388\t0.044\n1389\t0.504\n1390\t0.782\n1391\t0.944\n1392\t0.812\n1393\t0.807\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "5542e233-7c79-423c-9368-82328d18ff7c", "uuid": "da3bc065-4e07-440e-a1cb-a6518b600b59", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T06:01:21.607Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608_04511.txt", "content": "Yan, Y. and Duan, Z. (2024). Scoring time intervals using\nnon-hierarchical transformer for automatic piano\ntranscription. In Proc. International Society for Music\nInformation Retrieval Conference (ISMIR).\nYcart, A., Liu, L., Benetos, E., and Pearce, M. T. (2020).\nInvestigating the perceptual validity of evaluation metrics\nfor automatic piano music transcription. Transactions of\nthe International Society for Music Information Retrieval,\n3(1):68–81. 10.5334/tismir.57.\nZhang, H., Tang, J., Rafee, S. R. M., and Fazekas, S. D. G. (2022).\nAtepp: A dataset of automatically transcribed expressive\npiano performance. In ISMIR 2022 Hybrid Conference.\n\n\f15\n\nSupplementary File: Appendix\nSupplementary Material for A Dual Evaluation for Music Transcription\n\nA.1 OMR-NED DEFINITION DETAILS\nThis section gives the full definition of OMR-NED summarised in Section 2.1 of the main paper.\nOMR-NED defines edit operations over music-notation\nsymbols. These symbols include note and rest properties, such as pitch, accidentals, note heads, beams, flags,\ndots, ties, articulations, and ornaments, as well as nonnote score objects such as clefs, key signatures, time\nsignatures, dynamics, slurs, lyrics, directions, chord symbols, and other notation elements. The metric is therefore\nintended to measure differences in the sheet music that\nwould require manual correction by a user of an OMR (or,\nin our case, AMT) system.\nLet Ŷ denote a transcription and Y denote the reference sheet music. Consider a transformation from Ŷ into Y\nthrough insertions and deletions of notation symbols; let\n̂ Y) be the number of insertions and D(Y,\n̂ Y) the number\nI(Y,\nof deletions in the transformation that minimizes their\nsum. (OMR-NED does not count substitutions as a separate operation; a changed symbol is represented as one\ndeletion and one insertion.) The per-score OMR-NED is\ndefined as\n̂ Y) =\nOMR-NED(Y,\n\n̂ Y) + D(Y,\n̂ Y)\nI(Y,\n,\n̂ + |Y|\n|Y|\n\nA.1\n\nwhere |⋅| denotes the number of notation-symbols in the\nargument; this normalization makes OMR-NED lengthaware by scaling the edit distance by the total amount\nof notation present in both documents. Lower OMR-NED\nindicates stronger notational similarity, and a value of\nzero indicates an exact match.\nFollowing Martinez-Sevilla et al. (2025), our implementation reports a micro-averaged OMR-NED over all evaluated files:\n∑ ̂\n∑\nI(Yk , Yk ) + k D(Ŷ k , Yk )\nOverall OMR-NED = k ∑\n,\nA.2\n∑\n|Ŷ k | + k |Yk |\nk\nwhere k indexes evaluated recordings and sheet-music\npairs. For readability, all reported OMR-NED values, in both\ntables and figures, are scaled by 100.\n\nA.2 SUPPLEMENTARY SYSTEM RAW\nSCORES AND RANKINGS\nThis section reports complete 24-system raw scores\nand ranks. Table A.2 reports the raw values for the\nselected notation, rendered-audio, preference-based,\nand learned-embedding evaluators, while Table A.3\nreports the corresponding rank positions. Table A.1 compares each MUSTER subscore with OMR-NED and human\n\nMUSTER score\n\nOMR-NED\n\nHuman playback\n\nMeanER\nPitchER\nMissRate\nExtraRate\nOnsetER\nOffsetER\nVoiceER\nHandER\nScaleErr\n\n0.383\n−0.345\n−0.219\n0.044\n0.504\n0.782\n0.944\n0.812\n0.807", "numLines": 100, "startLine": 1294, "totalLines": 3822}}}
